<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hawiye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hawiye are one of the major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan families, living mainly in central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, including Mogadishu, and in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and Kenya. They are divided into many clans, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgaal">Abgaal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a>. Hawiye groups overthrew Ajuran rule in the late 17th century and have dominated the politics of Mogadishu since 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hawiye.jpg" alt="Hawiye" width="320" height="111" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carte Ortelius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hawiye_Kingdom_of_the_North_East_in_1570.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hawiye trace their lineage to Sheikh Ahmed bin Abdulrahman bin Uthman, known as Sheikh Hawiye. Oral tradition and Arabic hagiologies remember him as a saint called Hawi al-Ulum, conservator of knowledge, a name shortened in time to Hawiye, and carry his genealogy back to Aqeel bin Abu Talib. Some scholars regard such claims as historically untenable. The anthropologist Ioan Lewis held that traditions of descent from noble Arab families are most probably figurative expressions of the importance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> society. Tradition places his tomb at Qundhuro in the Haraghe region, where his eldest son Karanle is buried beside him.</p><p>The 12th-century writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Al-Idrisi</a> placed the Hawiye on the coast between Ras Hafun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Merca</a> and in the lower basin of the Shabelle river, the first documentary reference to a specific <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> group. Ibn Sa'id, who lived from 1214 to 1274, called Merca their capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 13th century the Hawiye, with the Rahanweyn, came under the Ajuran Empire, whose domain reached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> in the north to Qelafo in the west and Kismayo in the south. The Ajuran are themselves said to descend from the Jambelle branch of the Hawiye, and Lee Cassanelli describes Ajuran power as resting on spiritual pre-eminence and Hawiye kinship solidarity.</p><p>According to local oral tradition as Cassanelli reports it, the Hiraab Imamate was an alliance of closely related groups of the Gorgaarte division. Its founders rebelled because the Ajuran rulers had grown proud, neglected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> law and imposed heavy taxes. With Mogadishu as its capital, the imamate ruled from the Shabelle valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benadir">Benadir</a> to the arid lands of Mudug for at least two centuries. The explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kirk">John Kirk</a>, visiting in 1873, met Imam Mahmood at Mogadishu and saw about twenty large dhows there and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Merca</a> loading grain, much of it for Zanzibar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At independence in 1960 the first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-abdullah-osman">Aden Adde</a>, the first prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-issa">Abdullahi Issa</a>, and the founder of the army, Daud Abdulle Hirsi, were all Hawiye. Hawiye leaders formed the United Somali Congress in opposition to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>, whom General Mohamed Farrah Aidid, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a>, overthrew in 1991. From late 1991 Mogadishu was divided between the factions of Aidid and of President Ali Mahdi Muhammad, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgaal">Abgaal</a>. The stalemate lasted until 2006, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-courts-union">Islamic Courts Union</a>, a predominantly Hawiye organisation, ended the rule of the factional warlords, before an Ethiopian intervention overthrew it. The Hiraab hereditary leadership survives to this day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hawiye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…combining religious legitimacy with control of water, and its legacy shaped the political geography of southern Somalia: the clans that replaced it, including the <b>Hawiye</b> groups behind the Hiraab Imamate and the Geledi, inherited its territories, its wells and many of its ways of managing land and water.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 17th century the Hiraab clans of the <b>Hawiye</b>, among them the Abgaal and Habar Gidir, revolted against the Ajuran Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hiraab-imamate">Hiraab Imamate</a></li></ul></section>
